\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Optimization sensitivity of the rebuilt \textsc  {UltraFeedback} solve at $\eta _t=0.05$. Every row is a full dual solve on the same frozen tensor and differs in one setting. Opponent entropy is measured over the four comparators ($\ln 4=1.386$ is uniform), the fixed-point residual measures policy--opponent consistency in \eqref  {eq:fixed-opponent-update}, and the dual residual is $\|\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle s$}\mathaccent "0362{s}-\lambda ^{-1}\|_\infty $. Two things are worth reading off. The opponent budget $R$ does not change the solution at this step size --- it only sharpens the fixed-point residual --- so the residual of $0.9999$ reported for $R=1$ elsewhere in this paper is a consequence of the degenerate step size at $\eta _t=1$, not an independent defect. And the surplus rises monotonically as the opponent is weakened, reaching its maximum when $\beta \to \infty $ makes the opponent exactly uniform: surpluses measured against different opponents are not comparable to each other.}}{30}{table.132}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:optimization-ablation}{{13}{30}{Optimization sensitivity of the rebuilt \textsc {UltraFeedback} solve at $\eta _t=0.05$. Every row is a full dual solve on the same frozen tensor and differs in one setting. Opponent entropy is measured over the four comparators ($\ln 4=1.386$ is uniform), the fixed-point residual measures policy--opponent consistency in \eqref {eq:fixed-opponent-update}, and the dual residual is $\|\widehat s-\lambda ^{-1}\|_\infty $. Two things are worth reading off. The opponent budget $R$ does not change the solution at this step size --- it only sharpens the fixed-point residual --- so the residual of $0.9999$ reported for $R=1$ elsewhere in this paper is a consequence of the degenerate step size at $\eta _t=1$, not an independent defect. And the surplus rises monotonically as the opponent is weakened, reaching its maximum when $\beta \to \infty $ makes the opponent exactly uniform: surpluses measured against different opponents are not comparable to each other}{table.132}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Selecting the proximal step size $\eta _t$ on a training-side criterion. For each candidate the dual of \eqref  {eq:dual-update} is solved to the opponent fixed point on that panel's frozen tensor, and the concentration of the resulting exact inner solution is read off. The rule is fixed in advance: take the largest $\eta _t$ whose pooled policy keeps at least $0.9$ of the uniform entropy, so that the $(1/\eta _t)$ trust region still binds. No evaluation prompt is decoded. The released $\eta _t=1$ is off this table entirely: at $\eta _t=1$ the product $\eta _t\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _k\lambda _k$ is $25$ on a four-response pool and the inner solution is a point mass, so the regression target of \eqref  {eq:regression-loss} is built from a degenerate pooled policy.}}{30}{table.134}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:eta-selection}{{14}{30}{Selecting the proximal step size $\eta _t$ on a training-side criterion. For each candidate the dual of \eqref {eq:dual-update} is solved to the opponent fixed point on that panel's frozen tensor, and the concentration of the resulting exact inner solution is read off. The rule is fixed in advance: take the largest $\eta _t$ whose pooled policy keeps at least $0.9$ of the uniform entropy, so that the $(1/\eta _t)$ trust region still binds. No evaluation prompt is decoded. The released $\eta _t=1$ is off this table entirely: at $\eta _t=1$ the product $\eta _t\sum _k\lambda _k$ is $25$ on a four-response pool and the inner solution is a point mass, so the regression target of \eqref {eq:regression-loss} is built from a degenerate pooled policy}{table.134}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Exact finite-pool solutions under each aggregation rule, evaluated with all three welfare functionals. Every solve shares the panel's tensor, $\beta $, disagreement point, response pool, $\eta _t$, dual budget and opponent fixed point ($R=8$, damping $0.9$; residuals below $10^{-9}$ throughout). The Nash dual carries raw $\lambda $ with $\|\lambda \|_1=\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _k 1/s_k$, while the utilitarian rule sits at $\lambda =1$ and the max-min rules on the simplex, so the control rules are rescaled to the Nash arm's $\|\lambda \|_1$ on the same panel: without that the rules take proximal steps differing by a factor of $14$ to $55$ and the comparison is about step size rather than about the direction of the weight vector. Nash itself is never rescaled --- its magnitude is fixed by $\lambda _k=1/s_k$. The fixed-reference row is $\beta \to \infty $, whose surplus is measured against a collapsed opponent and is therefore not comparable to the rows above it.}}{31}{table.143}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:matched-welfare}{{15}{31}{Exact finite-pool solutions under each aggregation rule, evaluated with all three welfare functionals. Every solve shares the panel's tensor, $\beta $, disagreement point, response pool, $\eta _t$, dual budget and opponent fixed point ($R=8$, damping $0.9$; residuals below $10^{-9}$ throughout). The Nash dual carries raw $\lambda $ with $\|\lambda \|_1=\sum _k 1/s_k$, while the utilitarian rule sits at $\lambda =1$ and the max-min rules on the simplex, so the control rules are rescaled to the Nash arm's $\|\lambda \|_1$ on the same panel: without that the rules take proximal steps differing by a factor of $14$ to $55$ and the comparison is about step size rather than about the direction of the weight vector. Nash itself is never rescaled --- its magnitude is fixed by $\lambda _k=1/s_k$. The fixed-reference row is $\beta \to \infty $, whose surplus is measured against a collapsed opponent and is therefore not comparable to the rows above it}{table.143}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Regression-realization diagnostics for the pairwise target in \eqref  {eq:regression-loss}, on the training pairs and on a prompt-disjoint held-out split of the same pair set. The zero-predictor MSE is $\mathbb  {E}[Y^2]$ for $Y=\eta _t\DOTSB \sum@ \slimits@ _k\lambda _k Z_{t,k}$, attained by $\pi =\pi _t$, so a normalized MSE below $1$ means the fitted policy beats doing nothing. $\eta _t$ sets the size of that target: the target RMS column is $\eta _t\|Y\|_{\rm  rms}$. Sign agreement is reported on the pairs whose target is non-zero. The last row is a control in which $\mu $ is precomputed twice and the two passes differenced, which bounds the measurement error of every entry above it. Nothing here uses evaluation data: the selected $\eta _t$ is the one with the lower held-out normalized MSE on its own panel, and it is $1.0$ on all three.}}{31}{table.145}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:regression-realization}{{16}{31}{Regression-realization diagnostics for the pairwise target in \eqref {eq:regression-loss}, on the training pairs and on a prompt-disjoint held-out split of the same pair set. The zero-predictor MSE is $\E [Y^2]$ for $Y=\eta _t\sum _k\lambda _k Z_{t,k}$, attained by $\pi =\pi _t$, so a normalized MSE below $1$ means the fitted policy beats doing nothing. $\eta _t$ sets the size of that target: the target RMS column is $\eta _t\|Y\|_{\rm rms}$. Sign agreement is reported on the pairs whose target is non-zero. The last row is a control in which $\mu $ is precomputed twice and the two passes differenced, which bounds the measurement error of every entry above it. Nothing here uses evaluation data: the selected $\eta _t$ is the one with the lower held-out normalized MSE on its own panel, and it is $1.0$ on all three}{table.145}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces Disagreement between objectives on the frozen response pools. For each prompt, every learner sample is scored by its mean margin against the reference pool under each objective; the weak no-Condorcet rate is the share of prompts on which no sample is top under \emph  {every} objective, with ties counted as agreement, and ranking disagreement is the mean pairwise Kendall-tau distance between the objective-wise orderings.}}{31}{table.147}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:objective-disagreement}{{17}{31}{Disagreement between objectives on the frozen response pools. For each prompt, every learner sample is scored by its mean margin against the reference pool under each objective; the weak no-Condorcet rate is the share of prompts on which no sample is top under \emph {every} objective, with ties counted as agreement, and ranking disagreement is the mean pairwise Kendall-tau distance between the objective-wise orderings}{table.147}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces \textsc  {UltraFeedback} prompts split at the median ranking disagreement, with the adaptive game ($\beta {=}0.25$) and fixed-reference ($\beta \to \infty $) duals solved on each half to the same opponent fixed point. Bargaining is harder where the objectives disagree, but the surplus gap between the two representations does not widen there. The two surpluses are \emph  {not} comparable to each other: each is measured against its own opponent, and $\beta \to \infty $ collapses that opponent onto $\pi _{\mathrm  {ref}}$.}}{32}{table.149}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cycle-conditioned}{{18}{32}{\textsc {UltraFeedback} prompts split at the median ranking disagreement, with the adaptive game ($\beta {=}0.25$) and fixed-reference ($\beta \to \infty $) duals solved on each half to the same opponent fixed point. Bargaining is harder where the objectives disagree, but the surplus gap between the two representations does not widen there. The two surpluses are \emph {not} comparable to each other: each is measured against its own opponent, and $\beta \to \infty $ collapses that opponent onto $\piref $}{table.149}{}}
